A||||4||BAM-BAM||CV joints?||||5||
Z||000000||BAM-BAM||04-25-2000||12:54 AM||||   Hey guys, I'm about ready to tackle the front end and when I get that done I am going to start puting together the neccessaries for the rear suspension. While I'm there I am going to replace the CV Joints and boots. Just exactly how difficult is this project and how accurate is the Haynes and Chiltons manuals? Is there anything that they have conveniently omitted?<BR>I like doing these things myself and have the mechanical aptitude to get them done. Just need to know whats in store for me.<BR> Again, any info os greatly appriciated.<P> Thanx, BAM-BAM  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||5
Z||000001||DiggityDan||04-25-2000||01:18 AM||||CV joints are easy.....all you need is a big socket to get the spindle nut. Loosen the lower ball joint. Take a big pry bar and pry the control arm down until the ball joint pops out of the spindle. Take your big pry bar and carefully pry on the inside CV joint. Automatic transmissions are easier than manual transmissions, the clips inside the transmissions are designed a little differently. Might as well replace the output shaft seals while you are that far into it if they look like they might leak a little.||||reg||1
Z||000002||DiggityDan||04-25-2000||01:19 AM||||.....oh yeah, you have to remove the tie rod from the spindle as well, other than that, not much else to it.||||reg||1
Z||000003||ggarsk||04-25-2000||02:32 AM||||Do a search,there is a full topic on changing the axles. ||||reg||1
Z||000004||batboy||04-25-2000||10:17 AM||||I wouldn't mess with changing the CV joints and boots, it's almost cheaper and certainly much easier to replace the whole driveaxles as a unit. Also, carefully pry the axles out at the tranny case once everything is loose. Yanking it out at the wheel might pull the CV joint apart. If you're replacing it, maybe no big deal, but there is a core charge an the axle and some places might hard time you if the old ones are badly tore up.||||reg||1
